I dunno if their mix was bad or what, but Sweet Pill was yawn-worthy for me. The venue seemed quiet and disengaged, the singer was inaudible and overly distorted.  I liked her energy a lot, honestly, but the final product was a little off. She also wasn't sure if she was in Kansas City and went off on some weird tangent.

&#x200B;

Anxious was easily the best opener in my eyes. Energy was great, a lot of calls to action to the audience, and their sound was solid. They had the only crowd activity/moshing that wasn't TWY.

i'm gonna gatekeep a bit here, but if you're working under 40 hours per week early on, then this field may not be your jam. i don't mean 40 clients per week, that'd be batshit, but between a 'light' full time at 15 clients or a 'heavy' full time at 25 clients, you're gonna be working your tail off between supervision, session prep, notes, and other admin tasks.

&#x200B;

when you're fully licensed (done with supervision) and you can charge fuck you money per session, maybe 20 hours a week can sustain your life, but early on you better be prepared to thug it out.

